{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310639,"nonce":"yvy1cTrjnDnzEPKcyMqSP+ouEbA=","title":"FIXED in commit 139c4f5 (2026-06-05). The content-dependent SIGTRAP at llama-chat decode was a build_ptr_add-vs-build_gep semantic mismatch ...","message":"The bug was in `compiler/src/stdlib/qtensor.rs::build_qtensor_fused_qkv_matmul`.\nThe wrapper allocated a 32-byte `HaxeArray` struct and tried to initialise\nits four 8-byte fields via:\n\n```rust\nlet off24 = builder.const_i64(24);\nlet f_es = builder.ptr_add(arr_ptr, off24, ptr_i64.clone());\nbuilder.store(f_es, eight);\n```\n\n`build_ptr_add(ptr, offset, ty)` treats `offset` as an ELEMENT INDEX\nscaled by `sizeof(ty.pointee)` (same as LLVM GEP). With pointee=I64 the\nscale is 8, so `ptr_add(arr_ptr, 24, Ptr\u003cI64\u003e)` lowers to\n`arr_ptr + 24 * 8 = arr_ptr + 192` — 160 bytes past the 32-byte struct.\nThe `elem_size` field at offset 24 stayed at the freshly-zeroed value\nfrom `malloc`.\n\nCascade:\n1. `haxe_array_push_i64` reads `arr.elem_size = 0`\n2. Computes `new_size = INITIAL_CAPACITY * 0 = 0`\n3. Calls `alloc(Layout::from_size_align_unchecked(0, 8))`\n4. macOS returns the zero-size sentinel `0x2000000000000000`\n5. Stores sentinel as `arr.ptr`\n6. `triple[0]` deref hits the sentinel\n7. Without MallocStackLogging: tensor.scale's subsequent `alloc_tensor`\n   walks corrupted freelist → `brk #0x1` in\n   `_xzm_xzone_malloc_freelist_outlined`\n8. With MallocStackLogging: the deref itself raises `EXC_BAD_ACCESS`\n   at `0x2000000000000020` (loading `t.numel` at offset 0x20)\n\nFix: pass element indices (0/1/2/3) instead of byte offsets\n(0/8/16/24). Cranelift scales them by 8 to produce the intended\nbyte offsets 0/8/16/24.\n\nSymmetric code in `lower_array_literal` (hir_to_mir.rs:32277) uses\n`build_gep` with indices 0..3 and was always correct. This MIR wrapper\nwas the only `PtrAdd`-based offender.\n\n\n- 10/10 cold runs of \"A short story about Mark Zuckerberg and Facebook\"\n  at 50 tokens — PASS, 20-22 tok/s decode\n- 3/3 at 200 tokens — PASS, 22-24 tok/s\n- 128/128 haxe regression PASS\n- tiny-transformer PASS\n- llama-chat canonical \"Paris\" MATCH preserved\n\n\nThe investigation spanned multiple sessions. Forensic infrastructure\nthat proved load-bearing (all preserved in tree):\n\n1. **sigaction+ucontext PC capture** (commit 73564ab) — the legacy\n   signal handler couldn't unwind past `_sigtramp` on macOS arm64.\n   Reading PC + FP from the ucontext_t directly + walking the frame-\n   pointer chain (`walk_fp_chain_8`) gave us a real backtrace.\n2. **dladdr symbol resolution** (commit 73564ab) — converted raw PCs\n   to `rayzor_tensor_scale +0x4c → alloc_tensor +0x4d8 → brk`.\n3. **Per-PID JIT symbol map** (commit 73564ab) — pinned the JIT\n   caller PCs to `GQAttention.forward +0x478` etc.\n4. **lldb under MallocStackLogging** — exposed a SECOND crash\n   signature (`EXC_BAD_ACCESS at 0x2000000000000020`) that pointed\n   at the bogus value rather than the deferred freelist trap. This\n   was the key — both signatures came from the same root cause but\n   the MSL one was immediately actionable.\n5. **lldb memory dump** of the triple HaxeArray showed the\n   `elem_size=0` anomaly directly.\n6. **Runtime assertion in `haxe_array_push`** (\n   `runtime/src/haxe_array.rs:435-441`, then reverted) confirmed\n   `elem_size=0` at the first push call from `GQAttention.forward +0x478`.\n7. **Code review of the MIR wrapper** found the `PtrAdd` vs `GEP`\n   semantic mismatch.\n\n\nThe earlier session-end bisection of fast paths via env-var gates was\ncorrect: none of them were the cause. The bug was always in the\nHaxeArray init for the triple. The ARRAY itself is what every config\ngoes through (every prompt that exercises QTensor.fusedQkvMatmul), so\ndisabling any specific fast path didn't avoid it. The reason short\nprompts (\"Paris\") often worked was timing: the cascade from the\nzero-size sentinel store doesn't always reach a crashing kernel by\nthe time the model EOSes.\n\n\n- [[bugs-llama-chat-streaming-heap-corruption]] — historical framing\n- [[project-session-2026-06-compiler-arc]] — the long arc that finally\n  ended here\n- [[bugs-llama-chat-cached-run-crash]] — same root cause, different\n  manifestation in cold/warm runs\n- [[bugs-llama-chat-match-overstated]] — MATCH on canonical prompt\n  held throughout; the SIGTRAP was orthogonal to numerical correctness\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_llama_chat_heap_corruption_confirmed` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}